readme.install -- Installing, running and controlling Services
==============================================================

Requirements
------------

- A POSIX system (Linux, the BSDs, macOS) and a C99 compiler (GCC or Clang).
- CMake 3.20 or later, with Ninja or Make.
- GNU Bison 3.6 or later and flex 2.6 or later, which generate the
  configuration file parser.
- Optionally crypt(3), for the encryption/unix-crypt module.
- An ircu2 network (Undernet ircu 2.10 or later): Services speak the P10
  protocol natively and support no other IRC server.

Building and installing
-----------------------

The build is described in detail in BUILDING.md, in the top directory.  In
short:

    cmake -S . -B build                  # or: cmake --preset default
    cmake --build build -j
    cmake --install build

Everything is installed into the data directory, SERVICES_DATA_DIR
(`<prefix>/lib/ircservices` unless set otherwise):

    bin/ircservices          the program
    modules/<type>/<name>.so the modules
    languages/               the message catalogs
    example-ircservices.conf the example configuration

Configuring
-----------

    cd <SERVICES_DATA_DIR>
    cp example-ircservices.conf ircservices.conf

Then edit it.  ircservices.conf is the only configuration file: it holds
the core settings (the uplink, how Services present themselves, files,
timers), the list of modules to load and, at the end, the settings of each
module.
The syntax and every core setting are described in readme.config, the
module settings in readme.modules.  At the very least, set:

- `uplink { host; port; password; }` -- the ircu2 server to link to;
- `serverinfo { name; description; numeric; user; }` -- the numeric must
  be unique on the network;
- in the module settings, `ServicesRoot` (operserv/main) and, if mail/main is
  loaded, `FromAddress` (mail/main) and `RelayHost`/`SMTPName`
  (mail/smtp).

Configuring the IRC server
--------------------------

The uplink must know Services as a server and give them U-lined (UWorld)
privileges.  In its ircd.conf, with the names and password used in the
uplink and serverinfo blocks:

    Connect {
            name = "services.example.net";
            host = "127.0.0.1";          # where Services connect from
            password = "password";       # uplink { password }
            class = "Server";
    };
    UWorld {
            name = "services.example.net";
    };

The UWorld block is what lets Services log users in to accounts (ACCOUNT),
set channel modes and kick users without being channel operators.

Starting Services
-----------------

Services are started by running the `ircservices` program:

    bin/ircservices                  # detach and run in the background
    bin/ircservices -nofork -debug   # stay in the foreground, verbose log

On start-up, Services parse their command-line options, then read the
whole configuration: ircservices.conf and every file it includes.  This
happens once, before any module is loaded.  Any error -- a syntax error, a
missing required setting, a value of the wrong type -- is reported as

    ircservices.conf:42: syntax error, unexpected '}', expecting ';'
    ircservices.conf: Required directive `numeric' missing from block `serverinfo'

on the terminal and in the log, and Services exit.  Unknown settings are
reported the same way but only as warnings.

If the configuration is valid, Services open the log file, load the
language files and the modules (each module reads its block from the
configuration already in memory), detach from the terminal and connect to
the uplink.  Errors from then on are written to the log file only; if
Services appear to start but do not connect, look there.

Command-line options
--------------------

    -help                  List the available options.
    -dir=pathname          Use `pathname` as the data directory instead of
                           the compiled-in SERVICES_DATA_DIR.
    -remote=host[:port]    Connect to this server, overriding
                           uplink { host; port; }.
    -log=filename          Log to `filename`, overriding files { log; }.
    -debug                 Debug mode; give it several times for more
                           output.
    -readonly              Read-only mode: databases and log are not
                           written, and online changes are limited to
                           Services administrators.
    -nofork                Do not go into the background; log messages are
                           also written to the terminal.
    -noexpire              Disable all expirations (nicknames, channels,
                           autokills, session exceptions, ...).
    -noakill               Disable autokill checking (the list can still
                           be modified).
    -forceload             With database/version4, load as much as
                           possible from damaged databases instead of
                           aborting.
    -encrypt-all           Re-encrypt all passwords on start-up with
                           options { encryption; }.

Modules add their own options:

    -export[=filename]     misc/xml-export: write the databases as XML and
                           exit (see readme.xml).
    -import=filename       misc/xml-import: merge an XML file into the
                           databases and exit (see readme.xml).
    -clear-nick-email      nickserv/main: clear every nickname's E-mail
                           address on start-up.

Reloading the configuration
---------------------------

After editing the configuration, have Services re-read it with the OperServ
REHASH command or the SIGHUP signal:

    kill -HUP `cat ircservices.pid`

The configuration is parsed again from scratch, into a new tree, and the
core settings are checked against it.  If there is any error, it is
written to the log (the operator giving REHASH is told that the rehash
failed) and nothing changes: Services keep running with the configuration
they had.  Otherwise the new configuration replaces the old one, and then:

- modules whose loadmodule entry was removed are unloaded, in reverse
  order of loading (a module that other loaded modules depend on cannot be
  unloaded);
- the remaining modules re-read their blocks;
- modules with a new loadmodule entry are loaded, in file order.

A few settings are only used when Services connect, and changing them
takes effect at the next restart (a warning is logged): the uplink block
(host, port, password, local_address) and serverinfo (name, description,
user).  Changes to files { log; } and files { pid; } take effect at once.

Signals
-------

    SIGHUP    save the databases and reload the configuration
    SIGTERM   save the databases and exit
    SIGUSR2   close and reopen the log file (for log rotation)
    SIGINT, SIGQUIT
              terminate Services

Running
-------

Once linked, Services keep running until the uplink closes the connection
(for example after an /SQUIT), an OperServ RESTART, SHUTDOWN or QUIT
command is given, or a termination signal is received; the reason is
written to the log file.  The debug level and read-only mode can be changed
at run time with OperServ SET.

Services periodically save modified data to disk (every
timeouts { update; }, and on OperServ UPDATE).  The files are written in
such a way that a crash while saving leaves the previous contents intact;
even so, back up the data directory regularly.
